#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162855} :activity/id #uuid "e16c3950-01f1-5af7-ae8f-999ba2fcc9fa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555 :topic/id #uuid "a95c2c28-8d05-5d82-baa9-f51e879c16c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3127} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163230 :tutorial/content "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) <     0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form \n\n$$\nT(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if \n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) <     0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form\n\n$$\nR(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2\n$$\n\nis indefinite. Substituting ![](images/09/094a61c791563994cb520b40179d77546f776bfb9148851e211ec8ed326c279a.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0-0 \\\\  & =1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ![](images/f4/f4995691f0b24159baeb3cbb1ee51a9f44c72958c9288809f28f387d17b2810d.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+2-6 \\\\  & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6542a88-fba4-557b-ae08-48d95747b079" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4889 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6be8e550-b338-5637-a624-ef1b87eabd9b" :step/math-academy-id 30543 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161800 :question/id #uuid "f3f6f5b1-bc25-586c-9d4d-dfeb64cfa077" :question/math-academy-id "e-8262"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492064}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "ca8c0245-0936-52e2-ab35-4e10d9592791" :step/math-academy-id 30875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163241 :tutorial/content "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with a symmetric matrix $A$,  we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/85/8582c8b840b1e4696b9f83cb2c117d67e3b832e40b1ded716e0dcd23752c6f9c.svg)\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +6) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4,\\,-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) =  \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." :tutorial/id #uuid "c4b47113-5633-5c1b-bb17-e6ec70b5a550" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4952 :tutorial/title "Using Eigenvalues to Classify Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "2d8db3b7-e619-5958-9482-0acdf61cd3e6" :step/math-academy-id 30876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161801 :question/id #uuid "178e4bbc-718c-5b9e-843a-91914ef16f64" :question/math-academy-id "e-8263"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504680}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "20343d54-d701-5e59-9cab-48adc9d25870" :step/math-academy-id 30877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163242 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/c6/c6f4a56eed9073b3541e4398db9fc99f2e8ca94f651762e1f650bfe54865ca49.svg) for ![](images/16/1621ad9f5426c8fe19e2d5a00c5f92a60420aeaed7bfe623db70c9ab4e57527d.svg)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) \n\nAccording to **Sylvester's criterion**, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg) we have ![](images/03/039aff55ff839029e398761cdb94f09b8c2203413637d6f41ca9c680659cc8d2.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is odd, and ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is even.\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is negative-definite, then the leading principal minors alternate in sign, with the *first* leading principal minors being negative.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n\n![](images/bc/bc72eb8a57a1af3a4cc1526c071f7611131359d87b1ed56417e626aad662c9cb.svg)\n\nLet's compute its leading leading principal minors:\n\n![](images/84/844bf128565ccaeee211fcbeca2179b870901879303ce3e911956d65b468926f.svg)\n\nAll leading principal minors are positive. Therefore, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is positive-definite." :tutorial/id #uuid "721cf6ec-0dbe-515f-ae9f-63c855607e1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4953 :tutorial/title "Sylvester's Criterion"} :step/id #uuid "5cc719a5-ab07-5159-9a43-3394f7193bc5" :step/math-academy-id 30878 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161802 :question/id #uuid "fa658f82-1d0b-54a3-a172-4d59f03f629e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491984}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"} :step/id #uuid "d2fe4efb-5814-5b5d-a57c-b5cb76832908" :step/math-academy-id 30879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163318 :tutorial/content "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector ![](images/d3/d32277694c7bd75cf76ea413fba14b25a679f84e8194c07af201e103d23a1056.svg) \n\n![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ \n\nFor example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector ![](images/05/0592bc60943ac605d21672288a437282e3dddd52384b518f8869645f05540662.svg) \n\n![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic form is positive semi-definite, and\n\n- any negative-definite quadratic form is negative semi-definite." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f10e579-6a9a-5929-95df-033a67f11e7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6137 :tutorial/title "Positive and Negative Semi-Definite Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "2b84589b-e820-5864-af80-1c00cdcc8be5" :step/math-academy-id 37271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163319 :tutorial/content "The **leading principal minors** of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix are the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) determinants formed by the first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) rows and first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) columns of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) for ![](images/02/025dfd41d08c5cd679c6635316e6d92c7b612c1db8a0d5c88ca3633eb5dc3d0d.svg) The leading principal minors of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) are denoted ![](images/d4/d4d4f854b6186e5a619b024a48f4017756b1de3fcf8f3c90d80e5a251bc3de93.svg)\n\nNote that:\n\n- the first leading principal minors ![](images/e9/e983ef296ac0b20ed0beb7771d7616ab810886d775bd8e343f03f78c757ee678.svg) equals ![](images/1c/1caa82c7c23331cc78782a151b62bafd5f6d9acee12c7ae29dc74556ebc16c1c.svg) and\n\n- the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg)th leading principal minors ![](images/a2/a27bb42bcd37b2e4ba1416cff413d166b960a686a26ca5085da9be8661d8faf8.svg) equals ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by\n\n![](images/26/261bd51313b7630f2c14550d0318e1094a7915316c165e8555ddd77148b8b26d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is a ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) matrix, it has ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) leading principal minors. Let's compute them:\n\n- The first leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first row and first column of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg) \n![](images/9a/9ab81179136bdf16c09d64def927c7175884692ecd6f359143bb7333e91b3a2b.svg)\n\n- The second leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) rows and first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg) \n![](images/03/032d2edce1d92da3a0b49aa2e582f61896280571c9fd2c1cc277b7cc77af353e.svg)\n\n- The third leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) rows and first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n\n![](images/9d/9d1f53a40f9e6dfcb44f231287b67b70a42d1cd10dbf99c803517f21af7265eb.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8136146f-4191-5b4d-be2d-f33bf2b8d1f6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6138 :tutorial/title "The Leading Principal Minors of a Square Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ecf1e76d-67dd-5f72-a6ec-7fba2fc8aff6" :step/math-academy-id 37272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162898}}] :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162222}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504717} :activity/id #uuid "fb6cf230-95ba-52e0-b584-587b6c68c3e0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555 :topic/id #uuid "a95c2c28-8d05-5d82-baa9-f51e879c16c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3127} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347c0583-8380-5437-bea6-2f27bbcf1b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504120} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fd3eca-fb7c-56fe-9145-27b9896b8a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504350} {:answer/id #uuid "e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504432} {:answer/id #uuid "ea32c5e0-4b1f-5fce-827c-bcd543830e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504438} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb074c9-89ae-5600-83ef-9cf2870ac238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504432} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1da4cf-719f-5bbd-9c32-cdb26adbbe05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-94793" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abae4a38-c5fd-5f02-a772-5aa129094bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469734} {:answer/id #uuid "61d28482-9aa5-5496-9fe2-e88afd3b2c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469734} :answer-field/id #uuid "1888ce5d-ffbd-5ed4-8be1-6bd4f78e3048" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c36a82ef-f663-5517-a82d-dfb31295d4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469736} {:answer/id #uuid "1abaa9d3-1384-5eab-9d1f-4167841b8420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469737}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469736} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bab090-2cec-52a7-8c1a-ea25848b4605" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8053ba89-f2f1-5607-8419-7cfe23f95b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6d6721-833a-5366-b67a-9b57255478fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469739} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fa5bdc-7dac-5793-b27a-1eadf77bbfa1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a997acb-de91-5ebd-a6cc-103226757603" :question/math-academy-id "q-94787" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive (${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c25508-a03c-54ed-af75-528b36334d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464054} {:answer/id #uuid "f997d662-f0fe-5970-be50-6cced7ae7b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09da3cc-6896-57c1-b85f-50c6209f8fae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30af1cd-d4ae-5f09-b616-cad8cd88ec73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} {:answer/id #uuid "6256dff6-b788-5cb5-8d29-52c667534b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c4ab03-9594-5b6b-ae8b-d04de998a6a6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50467cc1-00c3-5d22-812d-d54f3f3960c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b1cda-a69f-5c84-8c20-b1e4a0fb21d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3999868-4945-5e38-b4fa-18fc7ca1a6ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139455" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420ec396-a215-5051-bcb8-10ed9acc95ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452101} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c27e94-c52e-5c96-8d89-053964a8a2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452101} :answer-field/id #uuid "83818ab7-8e39-5903-9ee3-959ef2554040" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c441b03-7a1e-59f5-9515-8053ce5355d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452103} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8eaa01-f5f3-50e0-9b13-165b38fc8277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452104} :answer-field/id #uuid "82bbe034-c71c-5eb5-aefd-fd09fd107a27" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897837ee-7319-5a6c-a781-a2bf0d97f197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452105} {:answer/id #uuid "317ed741-0f0a-53c2-9948-7ac37ceef348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452106} :answer-field/id #uuid "536fbf80-08b5-5e1a-a2ac-b0eae9c1cf2c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "43115d1d-8694-5a95-b413-4600fbd2bc68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94804" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e082f9d-d667-53bf-8ebe-ea0165b6dc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452107} {:answer/id #uuid "1b961c92-afa7-5358-90fd-24c675c9bd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452107} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d091f6-4c30-55e8-95e8-5336bea05763" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e233fee2-70e2-5e90-896f-04fbc826023e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452109} {:answer/id #uuid "a851a8d9-1e67-5532-b8dd-92c9d9b65acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452110} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be5e419-a12d-5643-8194-2d9a2b4897ec" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0fe63a9-b90e-5777-b826-3d8827fd649a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452111} {:answer/id #uuid "1aded71f-8051-53a7-97fa-e199f9608ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452111} :answer-field/id #uuid "42458164-cc6a-5528-9e47-6288386413d8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d5f731a-8eca-5400-88d7-dd10fc541120" :question/math-academy-id "q-94805" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=8$ and $det(A)<0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=8$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=8>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=21>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504718}}] :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504030}]}
